Timothy Spall is an actor with the  filmography that encompasses everything  from the cornerstones of Western  literature to the most modern mind  pieces in contemporary cinema and is  studded with awards and accolades of  every kind here are a few of the films  you might have seen him in spall might  play a different character in a very  different setting in Tim Burton's  adaptation of Sweeney Todd  but Potterheads will likely notice more  than a few similarities with Peter  Pettigrew his character Edel Bamford is  a wretched gleeful servant of Alan  Rickman's sinister Judge Turpin a man  whose power he clings to in the hopes  that some might rub off on him he is  deeply duplicitous luring innocents into  terpenes path through carefully deployed  secrets and lies  skimming whatever he can off the top  whenever he can get away with it and  he's not even the villain of this story  so much as he is a pitiful also-ran with  delusions of grandeur Beadle Bamford be  the judge as always my friends and  neighbors above all of these  similarities to Pettigrew however is the  sheer quality of Spall's performance no  one could have been as perfectly servile  as he is in his role nor so sumptuously  aware of his characters impotence spall  plays Bamford like a man who thinks he's  on the up and up with an actor's  awareness of the fact that he definitely  isn't as Winston Churchill in the King's  Speech small plays a larger-than-life  role yet in the context of this movie  it's not exactly a starring line spall  is instead a supporting player to Colin  Firth as King George the sixth in his  portrayal of the Kings  struggle to master his persistent  stammer as the movie winds on tensions  across Europe begin to brew and Spall's  Churchill finds himself unable to make  the declaration of war against Nazi  Germany by himself that is a duty that  falls to the king and the king alone and  so the training begins to take the King  from his impenetrable stutter to smooth  confident recitation the presence of  Spall's Churchill is a grounding force  in the film's final scenes in which King  George the sixth accomplishes his goal  and changes the world with nothing but  the power of his voice a microphone and  radio  broadcasting a rollicking ruthless  portrayal of arts and what fuels it the  critically acclaimed biopic mr Turner  sees small play the painter JMW Turner  accounts at the time depicted Turner as  consciously refusing the trappings of  Fame tying himself to ship's masts to  better paint sea scenes and never quite  fitting in with his colleagues at the  royal academy of art but his work was  utterly sublime capturing the power and  violence of nature as readily as its  Grace and tranquillity he was a deeply  complicated figure and small fills the  role like no one else would in a way the  actor takes to the role as Turner  himself took the painting passionately  and skillfully you've always been  preoccupied you're too preoccupied for  your own good sir spawn plays another  villainous henchman in Disney's  Enchanted the film sees Giselle a  woodland princess from the magical land  of andalasia trapped in New York City  when the Wicked Queen Nerissa pushes her  into a dimension spanning portal  Nerissa sends forth her helplessly  lovesick minion Nathaniel to keep an eye  on Giselle and generally do her bidding  spall gets to be quite a bit funnier  here than as peter pettigrew or beetle  Bamford and unlike with those other  characters ultimately gets to find his  Redemption our last glimpse of Nathaniel  and Enchanted finds him promoting a  self-help book about his experiences  with a long and ardent line of autograph  seekers waiting to meet him here spall  proves that not all of the servile  wretches need to be totally loathesome  he can make you root for them to unique  among these entries the BBC series  Timothy Spall at sea is about the man  himself he and his wife Shane are  dedicated sailing enthusiasts and this  BBC program follows them on their  journeys around the British coast aboard  their Dutch barge the princess Matilda  spall is a delightful guide to the world  of galleys and moorings his passion for  the freedom of life on the seas shining  through at every moment three series  have been produced somewhere at sea back  at sea and all at sea though Spall's  skill and expertise is an enormous part  of the show the foibles of sea life are  part of it as well  the couple infamously became lost in  2011 and had to be guided back to safety  by the authorities of the town of  Sheerness nevertheless Timothy Spall  sails on  spall teamed up with director Mike Lee  for all-or-nothing centered around three  working-class families and the traumas  and victories that define their lives  spawn plays Phil a father and taxi  driver caught in a web of addiction  dead-end jobs and family squabbling that  encompasses roughly a dozen people by  the time the film reaches its end  Spall's character lives a life that  edges on bleak yet he keeps away from  toppling into true despair something  that if anything makes the character  even more heart-rending his performance  has his feet planted firmly in moments  of extreme dysfunction and steadfast  love and honesty but catapults all or  nothing into greatness it's easy to see  why spall is one of Lee's go-to  performers together they find the eye of  the storm that is modern life in the UK  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit  the bells you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

